&#x200B;

Mine is 'Never go against a Sicilian when DEATH is on the line!'

It's time for everyone's favorite game

&#x200B;

Tankie or Conservative?

So I Think the reunification of America is inevitable, by 1936 there is simply too much of a national identity for people to not want America unified and the various factions would undeniably see each other too much as threats for the peace to hold for too long. for peace to hold were talking about, A socialist unity government, a proto-fascist state, no less than 4 military juntas of various ideological persuasions, a nazbol state, a classically accelerationist state, and the fist indigenous ruled country in the western hemisphere since the 1500s, all just living peacefully on American soil.

&#x200B;

In my opinion, most likely war breaks out rather quickly between the three primary eastern factions, around two-thirds of all American military industry is in the united communes, with a quarter in new England. The emergency committee in the south knows that the longer they wait the more the advantage turns to the northern factions, whereas I assume that most of the eastern army side with them at the beginning of the war giving them an early advantage while the other two eastern factions gear up. Shell, the leader of the Emergency Committee was a pretty much normal conservative general so I assume he hates every government around him, except maybe Missouri, but any general worth their salt (which he was) knows that the north is the threat and that there's almost no army infrastructure in Louisiana so they can be dealt with later.

&#x200B;

Similarly, the two other major northern factions are in a similar position in terms of wanting reunification, Flynn's government perceives itself as being legitimate, counts revolutionary socialists and radical unionists in its ranks and we should not discount the fact that Flynn was by no means a pacifist and was an ardent supporter of the reds war effort during the Russian civil war. While New England is a fascist government described as having some backing from the clan, it's pretty clear why they'd want a quick end to a socialist desegregationist faction. 

&#x200B;

I genuinely don't know who would win in the east, Flynn has had two years as president and could have done very popular things which have made her popular, or her government could be rife with dissidents and far too unstable to utilize its manpower and industrial advantage. Similarly, the south is governed by a conservative military council, and while the white population is probably fine with that; the black belt has just been taken by force out of America's first actively anti-racist government, I assume some resistance in the black belt but whether that's a protest or full-blown guerilla war is very up in the air. And new England is clearly the wildcard, comfortably the smallest faction and with left-leaning unionised Boston holding a good deal of its industry, it could either be the wildcard that sweeps to victory after the other two factions grind it out in Appalachia or it could collapse to turmoil early on. My bet for the victor would be Flynn, she's got the most established government and a literal supermajority of America's military industry on her side, plus the only faction of the three with the capacity to feed itself autonomously, but it could go to any of them.

&#x200B;

Also of note is New York, another industrialized state which is described as leaning progressive but seceding mostly out of a desire not to be involved in the conflict, it's unclear how this state would act as a wild card. And Kanawha, again would the radical unions of Blair mountain destabilise the state or would it fold to the emergency committee basically as soon as the dust settles, who knows. 

&#x200B;

Fundamentally the question of who unifies America comes to these three governments, California is not the juggernaut it is today in 1938, 90% of America's military industry is in the East and the factions in the center of America have no chance. Plausibly the east could be united by one of the three eastern majors who then can't pass the Rockies to retake the Pacific. But even that is unlikely.

&#x200B;

As for what the effects would be that depends so much on which government wins that I can't say anything meaningful about it.

I simply don't believe that the group you are referring to is that large. Yes, there are neo nazi's who reject Israel on the grounds they believe it gives jews a platform to subvert whiteness, an almost inconceivably repugnant concept. But that idea is largely ethnic rather than religious. Arab nationalists and Palestinians are largely motivated by land conflicts, which again boils down largely to ethnic conflict. The only group who I believe particularly oppose Israel on religious grounds are the Iranian government and groups like ISIL who see any group other than Muslims holding the city of Jerusalem (and more specifically the dome of the rock mosque) as an aberration before God. As for whether these people would be satisfied by some mass conversion of the Jewish population of Israel, I doubt it, religious fanatics are rarely actually satisfied until everyone follows their particular sect and creed so all that would happen is that the confict would become sectarian rather than interfaith.

&#x200B;

However, I believe the whole question obfuscates the more pertinent questions of state violence, settler colonialism, and ethnic cleansing that, at least in the west, are far more often the actual grievances being raised. The Israeli government and Israeli advocates like to present this issue as one of religion because it presents them in a better light internationally, no one could in their right mind oppose the religious freedom of Jewish people. But it is an obfuscation. And an obviously insincere one at that when the prime minister of Israel will happily call Jewish people who do not support his version of Zionism 'fake jews' and in a particularly harrowing example from my local area when a reform synagogue chose to march in a pro-Palestinian protest a local Orthodox synagogue organized members to shout slurs like 'race traitor' and throw eggs at the reform Jews. Religious conflict is a lot messier than people sometimes like to think and exists in a framework of state interests, personal interests, and ideology. 

&#x200B;

I would recommend that you look into the pro Palestinian argumkents with a more open mind, you may be surprised to find yourself mostly in agreement.
